<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the Sentry Mac app (plan §14.1).

  This file is deliberately one key long, and the reasoning for everything
  that is NOT here matters more than the one thing that is.

  WHAT IS HERE

  com.apple.security.app-sandbox = false
      The sandbox is off, and it is off as a stated fact rather than as a
      default that happens to be false. Sentry reads `libIOReport.dylib`
      (SystemMetricsKit/Bridges/IOReportBridge.swift) and the private
      `IOHIDEventSystemClient` API (SystemMetricsKit/Collectors/
      ThermalCollector.swift) through dlopen/dlsym, walks the IOKit registry,
      and spawns `/usr/bin/fdesetup` and friends
      (SystemMetricsKit/Security/SecurityPostureCollector.swift). None of that
      is reachable from inside the sandbox, which is precisely why this app is
      distributed with Developer ID + notarization and why the Mac App Store is
      not a fallback we are choosing not to take — it is categorically closed.
      Writing the key explicitly means a reader of this file learns that from
      the file, and means a future `ENABLE_APP_SANDBOX` default flip in Xcode
      cannot quietly sandbox the app and break thermal/power collection.

  WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY

  iCloud container / iCloud services / aps-environment
      Plan §14.1 lists these, and they would be lies today. There is no
      `CKContainer` or `CKDatabase` constructed anywhere in this codebase —
      `SentryKit/Sync/` imports CloudKit for its record *types* and its
      scheduling arithmetic, and `SyncPane`'s doc comment spells out at length
      that no upload path exists. An entitlement for a capability the code does
      not use buys nothing at runtime, and costs a provisioning-profile
      requirement plus an App ID capability that must be kept in sync forever.
      These get added in the same change that adds the first real `CKContainer`,
      not before.

  com.apple.security.application-groups
      The macOS side has no App Group on purpose; `WidgetSnapshotStore` uses a
      plain (non-group) UserDefaults suite on macOS for exactly this reason —
      see its doc comment, and the comment on the Sentry target in project.yml.
      Adding one here would require a Developer ID provisioning profile just to
      reach data the widget already reaches without it.

  Hardened-runtime exceptions (com.apple.security.cs.*)
      None are needed, and none are here. Library validation permits loading
      code signed by Apple or by the same Team ID: `libIOReport.dylib` and
      `IOKit.framework` are Apple-signed platform binaries, and every framework
      embedded in the bundle (SentryKit, SystemMetricsKit, and the SwiftPM
      product frameworks) is built and signed by the same Developer ID identity
      during the Release archive. So no `disable-library-validation`. There is
      no JIT, no writable-executable memory, no DYLD_* reliance, and no
      Apple-events automation anywhere in the app, so no `allow-jit`,
      `allow-unsigned-executable-memory`, `allow-dyld-environment-variables`, or
      `automation.apple-events` either. Each of those exceptions weakens the
      runtime protections notarization is checking for; adding one "just in
      case" is a real security regression in exchange for nothing.

  com.apple.security.get-task-allow
      Never in a Release build — it is an automatic notarization rejection.
      Xcode injects it for Debug builds via CODE_SIGN_INJECT_BASE_ENTITLEMENTS
      (which is what lets you attach a debugger), and project.yml turns that
      injection off for Release specifically so it cannot leak into a shipped
      archive. It is therefore correct that it does not appear in this file at
      all: Debug gets it injected, Release gets it withheld.

  com.apple.security.device.* / com.apple.security.network.*
      Sandbox-only keys. Outside the sandbox they grant nothing, so listing
      them would be decoration that reads like a security posture.

  CoreLocation (LocationService) needs no entitlement on unsandboxed macOS —
  it is gated by TCC and the NSLocation*UsageDescription strings in
  Sentry/Resources/Info.plist, which are already set.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
